01:11:20,440 --> 01:11:25,280
Recovery is important in sports, it's important on the psychological level.

586
01:11:30,050 --> 01:11:37,730
Okay, so exercise is good. The first part of the cocktail, I would argue, well, one of the most important ones.

587
01:11:39,730 --> 01:11:45,670
And yet, most people, not at Harvard necessarily, but most people around the world don't exercise.

588
01:11:46,070 --> 01:11:52,470
What are some of the barriers that we need to overcome in order to introduce exercise regularly into our lives?

589
01:11:52,470 --> 01:11:59,850
First, it is painful. It's not comfortable to exercise at, say, 70% of our maximum heart rate.

590
01:12:00,030 --> 01:12:05,010
It doesn't hurt that much, but it's more comfortable to sit in front of the television or play Nintendo.

591
01:12:06,590 --> 01:12:07,650
So what do we do?

592
01:12:08,110 --> 01:12:10,870
The first thing that we can do is divide and conquer.

593
01:12:11,730 --> 01:12:22,390
One of the things that especially perfectionists do is say, okay, so I'm going to start exercising, and to be fit, I need to do six miles a day, and that's what I'm going to start running, six miles a day.

594
01:12:23,070 --> 01:12:25,030
and of course what happens very quickly

595
01:12:25,790 --> 01:12:31,770
the next day you get up and you're hurt but you're a perfectionist, you're tough, no pain, no gain and you go out for a run again

596
01:12:32,370 --> 01:12:41,750
and what happens, you hurt even more the next day but you push through the pain and after a few weeks you get injured but even if you don't get injured

597
01:12:43,450 --> 01:12:47,690
the schema that is created in your mind about exercise is the following

598
01:12:49,890 --> 01:12:54,090
It is exercise equals pain.

599
01:12:55,190 --> 01:12:58,250
Now, our mind does not like pain.

600
01:12:58,970 --> 01:13:00,910
And it tries to avoid pain.

601
01:13:03,580 --> 01:13:07,020
So let's say you go on a holiday and you stop your exercise regime.

602
01:13:07,920 --> 01:13:11,240
You stop your exercise regime for a week or two weeks and then you come back.

603
01:13:11,620 --> 01:13:20,340
It becomes exceedingly difficult to go back to exercising. Why? Because your subconscious mind says, I don't want to experience this again.

604
01:13:22,550 --> 01:13:25,030
and then it prevents you, it paralyzes you.

605
01:13:26,630 --> 01:13:31,510
This is very often the procrastination that comes through because we don't want to experience that pain.

606
01:13:32,490 --> 01:13:37,330
A much healthier approach is to say, okay, so I'll start with walking for a mile,

607
01:13:38,210 --> 01:13:43,350
for 10 minutes or 20 minutes walk. Do it for a week or two weeks and then build it up gradually.

608
01:13:44,890 --> 01:13:48,330
And again, earn the sight of doing too little rather than too much.

609
01:13:48,910 --> 01:13:53,830
because then exercise, if I've built it up gradually, is not about pain.

610
01:13:54,310 --> 01:14:02,730
Over time, it even becomes sort of addictive in the positive sense of the word that we want to do it, that we long for it, so doing it gradually.

611
01:14:03,750 --> 01:14:05,450
Also, use distractors.

612
01:14:06,990 --> 01:14:13,990
When I jump on my trampoline, when I run outside, I have no problem, but when I jump on my trampoline, I have to have distractors. It is so boring for me.

613
01:14:15,250 --> 01:14:22,330
Again, some people can be on the bike for an hour and for them it's meditation and that's great.

614
01:14:22,850 --> 01:14:29,270
But many people need distractors, whether it's music, whether it's TV, whether it's my kid running around.

615
01:14:30,250 --> 01:14:32,750
But having something that keeps us going.

616
01:14:34,840 --> 01:14:36,020
Social support.

617
01:14:36,400 --> 01:14:48,100
The number one predictor of exercise success for the long term is do I do it with other people? Do I have the social support to do it? And once again, this is not right for everyone.

618
01:14:48,340 --> 01:14:57,520
There are certain people for whom the right thing is to do it by themselves. This is the time, especially introverts, where they enjoy just being on their own,

619
01:14:58,760 --> 01:15:05,180
just closing their eyes, listening to their music, or listening to their heart rate or whatever it is, heart beating.

620
01:15:05,560 --> 01:15:10,960
But they want time by themselves, and that's fine. But for most people, social support helps.

621
01:15:10,960 --> 01:15:20,160
Here is a study. This is done by Wing and Jerry, two psychologists.

622
01:15:20,580 --> 01:15:27,300
What they did was introduce individuals to a four-month program that included behavioral change,

623
01:15:27,680 --> 01:15:35,560
that included diet change, and that included exercise change for four months.

624
01:15:36,930 --> 01:15:47,170
The two conditions were either with social support, doing it with your friend and a family member, or without social support.

625
01:15:47,450 --> 01:15:50,410
Those without social support, who did it by themselves.

626
01:15:51,950 --> 01:16:00,290
76% completed the four-month program, and 24% were still doing it six months later.

627
01:16:01,110 --> 01:16:15,330
So 76% did it during the four months, 24% only, less than a quarter, did it six months later, kept up with the behavioral exercise as well as diet change.

628
01:16:15,330 --> 01:16:39,800
The group that had the social support, 95% completed the four months, 95% as opposed to the 76%, and 67% continued with the regime once the study was over for six months later.

